# The DISTRIBUTION default for "open this folder" — SynapseOS says synfiles.
#
# Installed to /usr/share/applications/mimeapps.list, which the mime-apps spec
# reserves for exactly this: the vendor's answer, at the BOTTOM of the
# precedence list. In full, highest first:
#
#   ~/.config/mimeapps.list          the user's own choice
#   /etc/xdg/mimeapps.list           the sysadmin's
#   ~/.local/share/applications/…    (legacy user location)
#   /usr/share/applications/…        THIS FILE — the distribution's
#   mimeinfo.cache                   whichever app happened to declare the type
#
# So a user who picks something else keeps it, an admin can override us for
# every account, and nobody has to be migrated: a system with no explicit
# choice starts opening folders in synfiles the moment this file lands.
#
# ── Why it exists at all ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Without a mimeapps.list entry the winner comes from mimeinfo.cache, which is
# just "who declared the type", and on SynapseOS the answer was KITTY —
# kitty-open.desktop lists inode/directory among its MimeTypes. Nothing else
# claimed it, so a terminal became the system answer to "open this folder", and
# every "Show in folder" in every application opened a shell at $HOME. It never
# reported an error, because kitty genuinely can display a directory.
# synui-open-folder learned to refuse a terminal handler; this fixes the same
# question for everything that does not go through synui-open-folder.
#
# ── Why it is here and not in the installer ─────────────────────────────────
#
# syn-install used to write inode/directory into the new user's own
# ~/.config/mimeapps.list. That is the user's file, and a default written there
# is indistinguishable from a choice the user made — it also only ever reached
# machines installed AFTER the line was added. A package-owned vendor default
# reaches every system on the next upgrade and un-installs itself with the
# package.
#
# SynapseOS Project — GPL-2.0-or-later
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
[Default Applications]
inode/directory=synfiles.desktop
